                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em Reeh-Schlieder theorem
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theorem"} theorem
            about
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+nets+of+observables"} local nets of observables
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag-Kastler+approach"} Haag-Kastler approach
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"}> AQFT
            ). It states that for certain nets the set of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vectors"} vectors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℳ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi Ω
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi ℋ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}(\mathcal{O}) \Omega \hookrightarrow \mathcal{H}
          %blockquote
            %p
              (the local
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+operator"} operator
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+observables"} algebra of observables
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℳ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}
              for a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bounded+set"} bounded
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subset"} open subset
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒪
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
              applied to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+state"} vacuum state
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag-Kastler+vacuum+representation"} Haag-Kastler vacuum representation
              for definitions and details)
          %p
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dense+subset"} dense
            in the given
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} state space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℋ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H}
          %p
            The Reeh-Schlieder theorem can be proven to be valid in the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag-Kastler+vacuum+representation"} Haag-Kastler vacuum representation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiom
            and called the
            %strong Reeh-Schlieder property
            in this case.
          %p
            The Reeh-Schlieder theorem is of central importance for the mathematical structure theory of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag-Kastler+approach"} Haag-Kastler approach
          %p
            Its
            %em physical interpretation
            is counterintuitive and therefore to a certain degree controversial: Intuitively one might expect that for a localized observable
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℳ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \in \mathcal{M}(\mathcal{O})
            the vector
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \Omega
            should be localized in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒪
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+complement"} causal complement
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒪
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \Omega
            %em localized states
            is nontrivial in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            and needs to be handled with care.
          %p
            Complementary statements about the asymptotic “vacuum-like appearance” of localized observables exist, too, and are commonly referred to as
            = succeed "." do
              %em cluster theorems
          %p
            In other parts of the physics literature, notably in the context of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+field+theory"} conformal field theory
            = succeed "," do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/state-operator+correspondence"} state-operator correspondence
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Schroer"} Schroer, footnote 14, page 34
          %h2#abstract Abstract
          %p
            We state the theorem for a vacuum representation on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetimes"} spacetimes
            as well as work concerned with dodging the theorem and its consequences by choosing an alternative set of axioms.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            All necessary definitions can be found at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag-Kastler+vacuum+representation"} Haag-Kastler vacuum representation
          %h2#statement Statement
          %p
            Both the statement and the proof we mention here refer to the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag-Kastler+vacuum+representation"} Haag-Kastler vacuum representation
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wightman+theory"} Wightman theory
          %p
          .num_theorem
            %h6 Theorem
            %p
              %strong (Reeh-Schlieder)
              %br/
              In the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag-Kastler+vacuum+representation"} Haag-Kastler vacuum representation
              the vacuum vector
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cyclic+vector"} cyclic
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separating+vector"} separating
              for every local algebra
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℳ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}(\mathcal{O})
          %p
            The following proof does not strive for maximal generality, for example we specialize to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo =
                  %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d = 4
            dimensions. The generalization to other dimensions can be done by the reader. We will comment on which part of the axioms of the vacuum representation are actually used below.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p First some preliminary observations:
            %p
              The covariance axiom says that we have a strongly continuous representation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              of the Poincare group, the SNAG-Theorem (see
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+measure"} spectral measure
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒯
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^4
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒰
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo ∈
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mn 4
                    %msup
                      %mi e
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                        %mi x
                        %mo ,
                        %mi k
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒯
                    %mo ≅
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{U}(x) = \int_{k\in \mathbb{R}^4} e^{i \langle x, k\rangle} \mathcal{P}(k) \qquad \forall x \in \mathcal{T} \cong \mathbb{R}^4
            %p
              We may restrict the domain of integration to the closure of the forward lightcone
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi cloV
                      %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} cloV_+
              by the spectrum condition. The spectral measure can be used to define operators for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi z
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                    %mo +
                    %mi i
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi ℛ
                      %mn 4
                    %mo +
                    %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z = x + i y \in \mathcal{R}^4 + i V_+
              via
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≔
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi cloV
                          %mo +
                    %msup
                      %mi e
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                        %mi z
                        %mo ,
                        %mi k
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    U(z)
                    \\;\coloneqq\;
                    \\int_{cloV_+} e^{i \langle z, k\rangle} \mathcal{P}(k)
            %p
              The dominated convergence theorem for spectral integrals (see
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+space"} Banach space
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %munder
                      %mi lim
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mn 0
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo +
                    %mi i
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\lim_{y \downarrow 0} U(x + i y) = U (x)
            %p
              Next we choose a vector
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℋ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u \in \mathcal{H}
              from our Hilbert space, a bounded operator
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℋ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H}
              and form the complex-valued function
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mi u
                        %mo ,
                        %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≔
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi u
                    %mo ,
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi A
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f_{u, A}(z)
                    \\;\coloneqq\;
                    \\langle u, U(z) A \Omega \rangle
                    \\,,
            %p
              which is holomorphic on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℛ
                      %mn 4
                    %mo +
                    %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{R}^4 + i V_+
              by definition and continuous on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℛ
                      %mn 4
                    %mo +
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mo +
                    %mo ∪
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{R}^4 + i (V_+ \cup \{0 \})
              by our last observation.
            %p
              We can define a second such function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℛ
                      %mn 4
                    %mo −
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mo +
                    %mo ∪
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{R}^4 - i (V_+ \cup \{0 \})
              by complex conjugation:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mrow
                        %mi u
                        %mo ,
                        %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mrow
                            %mi u
                            %mo ,
                            %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mover
                          %mi z
                          %mo ¯
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    g_{u, A}(z) = \overline{f_{u, A}(\overline z)}
            %p
              If there is a
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ⊂
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subset \mathbb{R}^4
              open such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mi u
                        %mo ,
                        %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_{u, A}
              is real valued on
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mi u
                        %mo ,
                        %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_{u, A}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mrow
                        %mi u
                        %mo ,
                        %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_{u, A}
              coincide on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              and we can invoke a suitable version of the
              %span.newWikiWord
                edge-of-the-wedge theorem
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/edge-of-the-wedge+theorem"} ?
              as stated on
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+geometry"} analytic geometry
              to conclude that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mi u
                        %mo ,
                        %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_{u, A}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mrow
                        %mi u
                        %mo ,
                        %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_{u, A}
              are the branches of a unique holomorphic function, that is holomorphic at least on a complex neighborhood of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %p
              Now the proof that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cyclic+vector"} cyclic
              for
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℳ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}(\mathcal{O})
            %p
              Choose a bounded open
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_0
              and a neighborhood of zero
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo ⊂
                    %msup
                      %mi ℛ
                      %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \subset \mathcal{R}^4
              such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mn 0
                      %mo +
                      %mi V
                      %mo ⊂
                      %mi 𝒪
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_0 + V \subset \mathcal{O}
            %p
              Suppose that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
              is not cyclic for
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℳ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}(\mathcal{O})
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℳ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}(\mathcal{O}) \Omega
              is not dense in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℋ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H}
              and we can choose a vector
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℋ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi v
                    %mo ≠
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v \in \mathcal{H}, v \neq 0
              such that
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi v
                    %mo ,
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi A
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\langle v, U(z) A \Omega \rangle = 0
            %p
              for all
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℳ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \in \mathcal{M}(\mathcal{O})
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℳ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_0 \in \mathcal{M}(\mathcal{O}_0)
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in V
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mi v
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi A
                          %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi v
                    %mo ,
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 0
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi v
                    %mo ,
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 0
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f_{v, A_0} (x) = \langle v, U(x) A_0 \Omega \rangle = \langle v, U(x) A_0 U(x)^{-1} \Omega \rangle = 0
            %p
              Now we see from our previous considerations that there is a function holomorphic in a complex neighborhood of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              that restricts to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mi v
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi A
                          %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_{v, A_0}
              on
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              and has therefore to vanish everywhere. That means the previous equality holds for arbitrary translations
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mn 4
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(x), x \in \mathbb{R}^4
            %p
              Recall that weak additivity holds in the vacuum representation. This together with the previous statement implies that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi v
                    %mo ,
                    %mi R
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle v, R \Omega \rangle = 0
              for all
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℛ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \in \mathcal{R}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℛ
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{R} \Omega
              is dense in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℋ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H}
              by assumption (see the axiom about the existence of a vacuum vector), we get that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
              must be zero, contradiction:
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
              has to be cyclic for
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℳ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}(\mathcal{O})
            %p
              Now the proof that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separating+vector"} separating
              for
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℳ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}(\mathcal{O})
            %p
              Choose a bounded open set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_2
              such that
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mo ⊥
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O} \perp \mathcal{O}_2
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℳ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℳ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}(\mathcal{O}) \subseteq  (\mathcal{M}(\mathcal{O}_2))'
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
              is cyclic for
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℳ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}(\mathcal{O}_2)
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℳ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}(\mathcal{O})
          %h2#preconditions_needed_for_the_proof Preconditions Needed for the Proof
          %p
            Since the theorem and its consequences are counterintuitive for the physical interpretation of the theory, it is worthwhile to take a look which axioms enter the proof: Is the theorem a true feature of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            or is it an artifact caused by ill chosen assumptions?
          %p The proof that the vacuum vector is cyclic given in the previous paragraph makes use of:
          %ol
            %li Strong representation of the translation subgroup and the spectrum condition.
          %p Note that we do not need the representation of the whole Poincare group, but really only that of the translations.
          %ol
            %li
              %p isotony,
            %li
              %p weak additivity and
            %li
              %p
                of course the existence of the vacuum vector itself and that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℛ
                      %mi Ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{R} \Omega
                is dense in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℋ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H}
          %p Now weak additivity can be proven to hold using isotony and additiviy.
          %p
            It is sometimes argued that the Reeh-Schlieder theorem - that is the part that the vacuum vector is cyclic for every local algebra - is somehow a contradiction to
            %em locality
            \. The remarkable fact in this context is that
            %strong the locality axiom does not enter the proof
            that the vacuum vector is cyclic. (It was used for the proof that the vacuum vector is also separating, though).
          %p For some further elaboration of this point see the paper by Halvorson cited in the references.
          %h2#consequences Consequences
          %p
            The fact that the vacuum vector is separating implies that there cannot be a number operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            associated to any bounded open set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒪
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            would annihilate the vacuum vector. This implies that the notion of
            %em particles
            in relativistic quantum physics cannot be quite as simple as in classical physics or in nonrelativistic quantum physics.
          %p
            More generally there cannot be a nonzero localized observable that annihilates the vacuum. This implies that the stress-energy tensor, if localized to a bounded region, cannot be a positive operator, since its expectation value in the vacuum is zero, in fact it cannot be bounded from below. This is sometimes mentioned as a pathology occuring in general spacetimes that do not have a global timelike
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Killing+vector"} Killing vector
            field, but in our context it actually holds in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            in the vacuum state.
          %p
            The fact that the vacuum vector is cyclic means that any arbitrary state in the vacuum representation can be approximated by measurements in an arbitrary small bounded open region applied to the vacuum vector. This fact is sometimes referred to as the existence of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+fluctuations"} vacuum fluctuations
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_1, \mathcal{O}_2
          %h2#related_theorems Related theorems
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin-statistics+theorem"} spin-statistics theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Osterwalder-Schrader+theorem"} Osterwalder-Schrader theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/state-field+correspondence"} state-field correspondence
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/GNS-construction"} GNS-construction
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stephen+J.+Summers"}> Stephen J. Summers
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Yet More Ado About Nothing: The Remarkable Relativistic Vacuum State
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Deep Beauty – Understanding the Quantum World through Mathematical Innovation
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0802.1854"} arXiv:0802.1854
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511976971.009"} doi:10.1017/CBO9780511976971.009
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
                , section 2 of:
                %em Notes on Some Entanglement Properties of Quantum Field Theory
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.04993"}> arXiv:1803.04993
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              Wikipedia:
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reeh%E2%80%93Schlieder_theorem"} Reeh-Schlieder Theorem
          %p
            The Reeh-Schlieder theorem can be generalized from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            to more general
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetimes"} spacetimes
          %ul
            %li
              Alexander Strohmaier,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rainer+Verch"} Rainer Verch
              %em Microlocal analysis of quantum fields on curved spacetimes: Analytic wavefront sets and Reeh-Schlieder theorems
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://xxx.uni-augsburg.de/abs/math-ph/0202003"}> arXiv:math-ph/0202003
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p On efforts to avoid the Reeh-Schlieder theorem and its counter-intuitive implications:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hans+Halvorson"}> Hans Halvorson
              \:
              %em Reeh-Schlieder Defeats Newton-Wigner: On alternative localization schemes in relativistic quantum field theory
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://xxx.uni-augsburg.de/abs/quant-ph/0007060"}> arXiv:quant-ph/0007060
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            The observation that the Reeh-Schlieder property describes what elsewhere is called the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator-state+correspondence"} operator-state correspondence
            is made explicit in:
          %ul
            %li#Schroer
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bert+Schroer"}> Bert Schroer
              , footnote 14 on page 34 of:
              %em Particle Physics and QFT at the Turn of the Century: Old principles with new concepts
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://cds.cern.ch/record/367824/files/9810080.pdf"}> pdf
              ,
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9810080"}> arXiv:hep-th/9810080
              \&amp;rbrack;
        .revisedby
          %p
